'''autopolygraph''' (â-tō-pol′i-gräf), ''n.'' [< Gr. αὐτός, self, + ''polygraph''.] An autographic printing process. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''autopsia†''' (â-top′si-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. αὐτοψία, a seeing with one’s own eyes, < αὐτοπτος, seen by one’s self, < αὐτός, self, + ὀπτός, seen (cf. ὄψις, sight): see ''optic''.] Same as ''autopsy'', 1.
{{smaller block|It is no small undertaking for a man . . . to begin a natural history from his own ''autopsia''. {{right|''Gilbert White.''}}}}

'''autopsic''' (â-top′sik), ''a.'' [< ''autopsia'' + ''-ic''.] '''1.''' Same as ''autoptic''.—'''2.''' In ''med.'', pertaining to or obtained by means of an autopsy.
{{smaller block|Undoubtedly the late King of Bavaria was insane, and the ''autopsic'' and the combined ante-mortem testimony to his insanity was not more confirmatory of mental derangement than that given in the history of Guiteau.
{{right|''Alien. and Neurol.'', VII. 533.}}
''Autopsic'' notes of cases of cellulitis.
{{right|''Thomas'', Med. Dict., p. 491.}}}}

'''autopsical''' (â-top′si-kạl), ''a.'' Same as ''autopsic''.
{{smaller block|Basing his opinion on the ''autopsical'' examination of fever patients.
{{right|''Ziemssen'', Cyc. of Med. (trans.), Supp., p. 561.}}}}
